Z||000010||ChadMan||09-12-2000||11:00 AM||||Hey Phantom, would you describe the article as pro-Fiero, anti-Fiero, or balanced?  If it's pro or balanced, I'll go buy the magazine - it's worth it to support anybody that will write about Fieros.<P>But if it's anti-Fiero, I'm not going to waste my money.  I've already read enough negative Fiero press to last a lifetime.||||reg||1
Z||000011||merlin||09-12-2000||02:04 PM||||On the negativity scale of 1-5, with 5 being the most negative, I would say this article ranks as a 4.  It speaks briefly about the Fiero’s history highlighting the supposed problem areas of the car (poor performance, ‘notchy’ 5-speed, poor reliability, and of course…  &lt;drum roll&gt; engine fires )  The article then goes on to describe the improvements made during the 86-87 run, with emphasis on the 88 as the ‘best’ year.  <P>Like many of these articles, the author has not done the appropriate research on the Fiero.  The author states that the ‘Pontiac spent $30m on new Lotus-designed suspension and brakes for the GT…’ for the redesign of the 1988 model.   Can you say rumor…  The author then identifies that hot exhaust manifolds were prone to cracking on the V6s when they came in contact with rain water requiring the engine to be removed for repair.  This is not a valid statement, in that it is not necessary to remove the entire engine to replace the exhaust manifolds.<P>The most positive commentary regarding the Fiero was in a side note ‘Why I own one’, which is basically a testimonial of a current owner.<P>If your tired of reading negative magazine articles on the Fiero, you may want to pass on buying this issue of ‘Classic an sports car’ and spare yourself some grief.||||reg||1
Z||000012||stimpy||09-12-2000||10:49 PM||||I read the article today and actually I felt it to be fairly accurate and not terribly defamatory. The Fiero is not the Pinnacle of uncompromised engineering, if it were, the majority of us jamokes would never be able to afford them.  The "facts" were about as accurate as to be expected from a British magazine, considering there are very few Fiero's in the UK and even fewer Fiero experts.<P>No, what really pissed me off was reading ALL of the "article" by David E. Davis in <I>Automobile</I> magazine. I mean, I loath the guy anyway, he makes Niles Craine look butch in comparison. The guy waxes eloquent on driving mocassins ferchristsakes! His snide comment regarding winking headlights on "every Fiero (he) see(s)" makes me want to rub his nose along every late 80's Honda Accord or early Mitsubishi Eclipse I see with non-functioning headlight motors. Any car with pop-up headlights will have that problem someday! The fact that the Fiero sold well during it's entire production run speaks a lot when considering the law of percentages. Which brings me to DED's Naderesque remarks about the tailhappiness of Fieros. If Ferarri sold 140000 308GTB/S's in 1984 at $8000 a pop, don't you think that you'd see more ratty Ferarri's driving around with their tails stuffed into snowbanks? <P>David E. is just pissy that the car was't made in Germany costing $50,000. If it were, he would sing the praises of its distinct character and Italianate styling. As it is, he can kiss my non-mocassin wearin ass!  <P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||09-13-2000||05:46 AM||||A Fiero friend can be found in Brock Yates from Car and Driver. If I can find it I will post it but he was another writer that wrote a scalding article about GM giving up on a good thing. He was also describing the Impala SS but as I remember he was very pro Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000014||ChadMan||09-13-2000||08:57 AM||||VERY well put, Stimpy!  Davis' pomposity was a running joke in Car and Driver - even when he was editor-in-chief there!<P>Yates is cool.  Another Fiero friend can be found in Peter Egan (Road & Track).  He wrote a really nice piece about an 87 GT 5-speed, comparing and contrasting it with the Ferrari Dino 246 and discussing what a great "grand touring" car the Fiero was.||||reg||14
Z||000015||DJRice||09-13-2000||02:27 PM||||Thats it!  Im putting my Corinthian leather mocassins and matching driving gloves in the bag of stuff headed for dontation to the church yard sale.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000016||Black88GT||09-13-2000||06:28 PM||||Brock Yates wrote a nice article titles "Fiero's, Corvairs, and alphabet soup nobodies" which I retyped and put on my webpage.  It just talks about how GM kills its young.  He seemed pro-fiero.<BR> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
